<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bagler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1196–<a href="/years/1217">1217</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Baglers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bagler were a faction in the Norwegian civil wars, formed in 1196 by opponents of King Sverre with the backing of the church, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Nicholas Arnesson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>. Strongest in the east around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslofjord">Oslofjord</a>, they set up rival kings and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiner</a> for two decades. A settlement in 1208 and the accession of Haakon IV in 1217 brought the factions together.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bagler.jpg" alt="Bagler" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Knud Bergslien · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birkebeinerne_ski01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bagler faction was founded in Skåne, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, by Bishop Nicholas Arnesson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a> and Archbishop Erik Ivarsson of Nidaros, around the pretender Inge Magnusson, nicknamed the Baglar-King. Its forerunners had supported King Inge I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and later King Magnus V and his father and regent, Earl Erling Skakke. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiner</a>, essentially a peasant faction, had been formed in 1174 to depose Magnus and Erling, and under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sverre-of-norway">Sverre Sigurdsson</a> they took control of much of the country. Sverre's claim to descend from King Sigurd II was doubted in his own time, and historians generally regard him as a pretender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Bagler drew principally on the aristocracy, the clergy and the merchants, above all the wealthier classes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viken">Viken</a>, where social distinctions were growing deeper. They favoured the church and its privileges and possessions. Historians read them as conservatives opposed to developments such as the growth of landless border men, the markamenn, who settled along the Swedish border, lived by raiding the old settlements and formed the chief support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiner</a>. Like their rivals, the Bagler rallied around a royal son set up to contest the rule of the other party's king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Bagler remained powerful in Hedmark and Østerdal even when Sverre held most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and their strength grew after his death in 1202. His successor, King Haakon III, died in 1204 and left a posthumous son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon Haakonsson</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamar">Hamar</a>, then under Bagler control; in 1206 a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiner</a> carried the two-year-old boy through mountains and forests to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a>.</p><p>The Bagler pretenders were Inge Magnusson until 1202, Erling Steinvegg from 1204 to 1207 and Philip Simonsson from 1207 to 1217. Under the pact of 1208 Philip ruled Oppland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viken">Viken</a> without the title of king, while Inge Bårdsson was recognised as King Inge II. Both died in 1217, and Haakon IV came to the throne under the regency of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skule-bardsson">Skule Baardsson</a>; in 1240 he ended the civil wars.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bagler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1130 to 1240 the kingdom was torn by civil wars between rival claimants and factions such as the Birkebeiner and the <b>Bagler</b>; Sverre Sigurdsson secured his throne with a sea victory at Fimreite in 1184.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Military</a></li></ul></section>
